WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.960
By the spring of 1950, only five short years after the last shots of World War II had been

00:09.960 --> 00:19.160
fired, the United States Army had been reduced to an active force of 600,000.

00:19.160 --> 00:35.760
A great number of these were scattered about the world on occupation duty.

00:35.760 --> 00:42.680
But the armed forces of the truculent Communists, Soviet Russia and Red China, remained on a

00:42.680 --> 00:52.400
strong wartime footing and in belligerent posture.

00:52.400 --> 00:56.280
Aggression was soon to come.

00:56.280 --> 01:22.680
Russians would be fighting against tyranny for the third time in 32 years.

01:22.680 --> 01:49.440
Korea, a

01:49.440 --> 01:58.480
rather little finger of land, but of key importance to the free world in the Far East.

01:58.480 --> 02:09.040
What happened here opened the next major chapter in the history of the United States Army.

02:09.040 --> 02:14.680
The war began here, at the 38th parallel.

02:14.680 --> 02:22.120
Just how did this imaginary line become the fuse for a three-year explosion?

02:22.120 --> 02:27.200
Before the Japanese surrender in World War II, we made an agreement with the Russians,

02:27.200 --> 02:29.920
who were then our allies.

02:29.920 --> 02:34.960
When surrender came on the Asian mainland, Russian troops would accept the surrender

02:34.960 --> 02:45.240
of Japanese forces north of the 38th parallel, and we would do the same south of it.

02:45.240 --> 02:52.240
The 38th parallel was chosen because it divides the Korean peninsula roughly in half.

02:52.240 --> 02:58.440
It was also agreed that free elections would be held, and the government of Korea returned

02:58.440 --> 03:01.680
to the Korean people.

03:01.680 --> 03:08.800
In August of 1948, free elections were held in South Korea, supervised by United Nations

03:08.800 --> 03:14.640
inspectors, establishing the Republic of Korea.

03:14.640 --> 03:21.560
Three weeks later, communist-controlled elections in North Korea, from which UN inspectors were

03:21.560 --> 03:29.000
barred, established the communist regime and divided the Korean nation.

03:29.000 --> 03:36.800
Not long afterwards, the communists decided their moment for conquest had come.

03:36.800 --> 03:42.840
In early summer 1950, life was good in the agricultural South.

03:42.840 --> 04:07.920
There was peace, time for living in the quiet, ancient way.

04:07.920 --> 04:23.440
Then suddenly, in the pre-dawn darkness of June 25, 1950, South Koreans were jarred awake

04:23.440 --> 04:25.480
by a living nightmare.

04:25.480 --> 04:50.160
Unprovoked and unannounced, the communist latest war of conquest had begun.

04:50.160 --> 04:53.440
Many thought it was the beginning of World War III.

04:53.440 --> 04:59.760
What might have been if we had not taken immediate action?

04:59.760 --> 05:04.160
President Truman ordered our Navy and Air Force to provide cover and support to the

05:04.160 --> 05:08.080
Republic of Korea.

05:08.080 --> 05:13.600
In the United Nations, United States delegate Warren Austin spoke for the indignant people

05:13.600 --> 05:17.440
of free nations everywhere.

05:17.440 --> 05:22.160
The armed invasion of the Republic of Korea continues.

05:22.160 --> 05:28.320
The Republic of Korea has appealed to the United Nations for protection.

05:28.320 --> 05:36.160
I am proud to report that the United States is prepared to furnish assistance to the Republic

05:36.160 --> 05:37.280
of Korea.

05:37.280 --> 05:46.480
Four days after the invasion began, the capital city of Seoul fell to the communists.

05:46.480 --> 05:53.920
All elements of the United States Army were airlifted from Japan to help check the Red

05:53.920 --> 05:58.000
Advance.

05:58.000 --> 06:01.440
They were greeted with cheers.

06:01.440 --> 06:06.600
But the cheering was soon forgotten.

06:06.600 --> 06:12.040
Our small forces found themselves greatly outnumbered by communist troops trained and

06:12.040 --> 06:19.120
equipped by the Russians.

06:19.120 --> 06:24.360
Our handful of troops fought a delaying action, buying precious time until reinforcements

06:24.360 --> 06:33.040
would arrive.

06:33.040 --> 06:38.080
We showed the communists that we had the will to fight aggression wherever and whenever

06:38.080 --> 06:45.000
it occurred.

06:45.000 --> 06:50.680
On July 10th, President Truman appointed General Douglas MacArthur, Commander-in-Chief of the

06:50.680 --> 07:09.560
United Nations forces, which were beginning to assemble.

07:09.560 --> 07:21.000
For the outnumbered troops in the field, it was fight, pull back, and fight again.

07:21.000 --> 07:24.280
More United States forces arrived in Pusan.

07:24.280 --> 07:35.400
Not nearly enough, but it was a beginning.

07:35.400 --> 07:40.240
General commanders like General Walton H. Walker and General William F. Dean found their

07:40.240 --> 07:47.280
task was a grim one and all too familiar historically, a holding action with no time and too few

07:47.280 --> 07:59.920
men.

07:59.920 --> 08:05.080
But American military leadership had only recently been bloodied and honed to professional

08:05.080 --> 08:07.320
keenness in a greater war.

08:07.320 --> 08:13.160
To reinforce and compliment the top leaders was a hard core of seasoned officers and enlisted

08:13.160 --> 08:18.920
men who had won their campaign ribbons on other fields of battle only five or six years

08:18.920 --> 08:42.960
before.

08:42.960 --> 08:48.240
Through July and August of 1950, our small forces were being pushed into a corner.

08:48.240 --> 08:54.520
The Pusan perimeter.

08:54.520 --> 09:00.040
We rushed more troops from Japan.

09:00.040 --> 09:06.520
Again, we were faced with an emergency buildup to fighting strength.

09:06.520 --> 09:11.360
We drew on our citizen soldiers, reserves, and inductees.

09:11.360 --> 09:29.240
In the year, our army would grow from 600,000 to more than a million and a half men.

09:29.240 --> 09:48.080
Other United Nations forces began arriving in Korea.

09:48.080 --> 09:51.920
British troops of the Sutherland and Argyle Highlanders.

09:51.920 --> 09:54.840
The international army was beginning to take shape.

09:54.840 --> 09:56.840
It was none too soon.

09:56.840 --> 10:04.640
Once again, time was running out.

10:04.640 --> 10:09.560
The Naktong River provided a natural defense line around Pusan.

10:09.560 --> 10:14.240
It was here that the guts and the stamina of the American soldier and his Republic of

10:14.240 --> 10:43.440
Korea allies and a protective wall of artillery fire held the communist forces at bay.

10:45.120 --> 11:01.840
The buildup of allied strength was rapidly gaining momentum.

11:01.840 --> 11:21.840
In Chang, September 15, 1950, the United Nations suddenly took the offensive.

11:21.840 --> 11:40.240
General Douglas MacArthur, the Supreme Commander, had conceived this daring amphibious strike

11:40.240 --> 11:57.840
along the coast of the Yellow Sea, just west of Seoul.

11:57.840 --> 12:06.640
The Marine landing force secured Wolmi Island in just 58 minutes and pushed on into Inchon

12:06.640 --> 12:22.440
itself, kicking in the back door to the enemy's defenses.

12:22.440 --> 12:23.960
Additional army forces poured in.

12:23.960 --> 12:43.640
Simultaneously, the breakout from the Pusan perimeter got underway in the south.

12:43.640 --> 12:53.840
In a week of heavy fighting, our forces drove 20 miles inland from Inchon to Seoul.

12:53.840 --> 12:59.160
More than 10,000 North Korean troops were dug in and waiting at every street corner

12:59.160 --> 13:29.100
with orders to fight to the death.

13:29.100 --> 13:34.600
The United Nations offensive rolled back the communist forces, sweeping northward past

13:34.600 --> 13:45.600
the 38th parallel.

13:45.600 --> 13:54.920
First Pusan fell.

13:54.920 --> 14:11.120
Then Wonsan on the east coast.

14:11.120 --> 14:30.920
Then Pyongyang, the North Korean capital itself.

14:30.920 --> 14:38.280
Psychological warfare played an important part.

14:38.280 --> 14:48.160
Loudspeaker messages and safe conduct passes for North Korean troops inviting them to surrender

14:48.160 --> 15:02.640
met with striking success.

15:02.640 --> 15:25.040
Then entire units stacked their weapons and awaited the arrival of UN forces.

15:25.040 --> 15:41.080
More army units pushed on toward the Yalu River, Korea's northernmost boundary.

15:41.080 --> 15:44.400
But there were signs of potential trouble ahead.

15:44.400 --> 15:49.680
Some of the prisoners taken in the north wore the quilted uniform of the communist Chinese

15:49.680 --> 15:55.320
army.

15:55.320 --> 16:05.480
November found elements of the 10th Corps on the frozen banks of the Yalu River.

16:05.480 --> 16:10.080
While they ate their Thanksgiving dinner, they could look across the river and see communist

16:10.080 --> 16:15.120
China.

16:15.120 --> 16:16.600
Victory seemed assured.

16:16.600 --> 16:22.080
There was talk of war's end by Christmas.

16:22.080 --> 16:25.640
But on the other side of the Yalu, Chinese armies were massive.

16:25.640 --> 16:34.480
A whole new war was about to begin.

16:34.480 --> 16:40.840
Late in November, two full Chinese field armies attacked across the Yalu, splitting UN forces

16:40.840 --> 16:42.560
and rolling them back.

16:42.560 --> 16:49.000
What President Truman had first called a police action had now become a major war.

16:49.000 --> 17:00.920
Our forces in the west pulled back to the south.

17:00.920 --> 17:05.640
Elements of the 1st Marine Division and the 7th Infantry Division, 10th Corps, along with

17:05.640 --> 17:11.640
Republic of Korea troops, cut off in the northeast, fought their way in sub-zero weather towards

17:11.640 --> 17:20.600
the port of Hongnam and evacuation by sea.

17:20.600 --> 17:33.360
One hundred thousand fighting men and their machines were loaded onto waiting ships.

17:33.360 --> 17:38.900
More than 90,000 refugees fled from their homes in North Korea rather than live again

17:38.900 --> 17:41.140
under communist rule.

17:41.140 --> 17:50.400
We gave transportation to freedom to as many of them as we could.

17:50.400 --> 17:56.360
By sea, the massive withdrawal moved southward, toward ports where the units could land and

17:56.360 --> 17:57.360
regroup.

17:57.360 --> 18:14.020
In the west, elements of the 8th Army withdrew south of the 38th parallel.

18:14.020 --> 18:25.200
The Chinese forces followed up with their massive New Year's offensive.

18:25.200 --> 18:28.460
Millions of Koreans choked the roads leading southward.

18:28.460 --> 18:31.000
They had seen communism in practice.

18:31.000 --> 18:41.420
Any hardship was preferable.

18:41.420 --> 18:50.780
Once again, Seoul changed hands as we pulled out, blowing up the bridges behind us.

18:50.780 --> 19:08.060
Far to the south, our units were regrouping.

19:08.060 --> 19:13.740
In late February, we made a fresh start, an Operation General Ridgeway graphically called

19:13.740 --> 19:15.820
Operation Killer.

19:15.820 --> 19:22.580
The Chinese armies held a staggering superiority of numbers, and Ridgeway's intent was simple,

19:22.580 --> 19:23.580
cut down the odds.

19:23.580 --> 19:52.940
Operation Killer did not end there.

19:52.940 --> 19:55.700
Operation Killer did just that.

19:55.700 --> 20:00.540
The enemy lost 10 men for every United Nations casualty.

20:00.540 --> 20:05.820
What had begun as an attempt to inflict heavy personnel losses on the enemy developed into

20:05.820 --> 20:18.140
a ground gaining operation.

20:18.140 --> 20:22.260
On March 15th, Seoul again changed hands.

20:22.260 --> 20:26.100
North Korean troops entered the deserted streets unopposed.

20:26.100 --> 20:36.820
The enemy had pulled out to the north.

20:36.820 --> 20:38.660
But not for long.

20:38.660 --> 20:48.980
Spring brought with it still another crisis, another massive communist offensive.

20:48.980 --> 20:54.100
The Van Fleet fortified the city of Seoul, determined not to lose it again.

20:54.100 --> 20:58.620
The enemy came on in human ways, to be met by a storm of fire.

20:58.620 --> 21:02.500
They lost tens of thousands, but still they came on.

21:02.500 --> 21:10.460
And then suddenly, they faltered, broke, and started pulling back, pursued by our armor.

21:10.460 --> 21:15.780
Again the communists had run afoul of two things, the immense firepower of the Eighth

21:15.780 --> 21:19.460
Army and the principles of logistics.

21:19.460 --> 21:24.740
They were simply unable to supply a large force for a sustained offensive.

21:24.740 --> 21:32.420
June 2nd, 1951, again we crossed the 38th parallel, a badly battered enemy withdrawing

21:32.420 --> 21:39.660
before us.

21:39.660 --> 21:45.180
Soviet UN delegate, Jacob Malik, frankly proposed truce talks, suggesting that perhaps

21:45.180 --> 21:48.940
a ceasefire could be discussed.

21:48.940 --> 21:54.340
On July 10th, preliminary truce talks were opened in the red-held city of Kaesong.

21:54.340 --> 22:04.420
They were to drag on for two long years, while the bloodshed continued.

22:04.420 --> 22:11.940
The lines of the fighting front became virtually stabilized.

22:11.940 --> 22:18.660
After 12 days, the talks in Kaesong were deadlocked over the issue of a ceasefire line.

22:18.660 --> 22:24.500
Then the communists broke off the talks.

22:24.500 --> 22:35.820
In the fall, they were resumed at Panmunjom.

22:35.820 --> 22:46.140
The fighting continued.

22:46.140 --> 23:04.580
Materials were expended daily and nightly in prodigious amounts.

23:04.580 --> 23:11.460
Air-to-air supply was a new military concept that came to full development in Korea.

23:11.460 --> 23:34.420
Airlift and airdrop techniques became a major means of supply in a large-scale combat situation.

23:34.420 --> 23:44.460
Later 1952, found the talks at Panmunjom deadlocked over the principle of prisoner exchange.

23:44.460 --> 23:49.500
While back and forth along the twisted length of the main line of resistance raged the bitter

23:49.500 --> 24:17.780
hill battles.

24:19.500 --> 24:30.220
In March 1953, the communist world was shaken by the death of Joseph Stalin.

24:30.220 --> 24:36.740
His successor, Georgy Malenkov, launched what he termed a worldwide peace offensive.

24:36.740 --> 24:45.580
Its influence would soon be felt in Korea.

24:45.580 --> 24:51.260
Within a month, the communists suddenly took us up on our standing offer to exchange sick

24:51.260 --> 24:55.780
and wounded prisoners.

24:55.780 --> 24:58.700
This was Operation Little Switch.

24:58.700 --> 25:05.340
It indicated that a ceasefire might come soon.

25:05.340 --> 25:13.660
Battle-weary men listened for word that the shooting was to end.

25:13.660 --> 25:22.820
On July 27, 1953, at the UN base camp in Monsignor, General Mark Clark signed the ceasefire agreement.

25:22.820 --> 25:30.940
A few miles away, the communists did the same.

25:30.940 --> 25:35.020
By August 5, Operation Big Switch was underway.

25:35.020 --> 25:46.780
Some 13,000 UN soldiers returned from Red prison camps.

25:46.780 --> 25:51.420
General William Dean, captured in the battle for Taejon early in the war, returned with

25:51.420 --> 25:55.900
his thousands of comrades in arms.

25:55.900 --> 26:01.780
It was then that the world image of communism received a telling blow, for two-thirds of

26:01.780 --> 26:08.700
the Chinese prisoners refused to return to Red China, and 35,000 North Koreans decided

26:08.700 --> 26:18.260
they'd rather live in South Korea.

26:18.260 --> 26:23.180
An armistice, however, is only a ceasefire, not peace.

26:23.180 --> 26:27.700
And in this divided nation, the need is for constant watchfulness.

26:27.700 --> 26:33.180
Unending preparedness still remains and may continue for years to come.

26:33.180 --> 27:00.460
This is true anywhere on earth where people face an aggressor.

27:00.460 --> 27:05.580
The communists had miscalculated in Korea.

27:05.580 --> 27:11.820
They could not bring themselves to believe that we would spend so much of materiel and

27:11.820 --> 27:19.340
of effort and of pain so far from home.

27:19.340 --> 27:24.660
That lesson was an important one for all concerned.

27:24.660 --> 27:32.420
Once again, the United States Army, the American soldier, had stood foremost in a massive military

27:32.420 --> 27:40.300
force on foreign soil, but not for conquest.

27:40.300 --> 27:46.140
As the years passed, we began to see that our preparedness would have to be large-scale,

27:46.140 --> 27:51.420
constant, flexible, and adaptable.

27:51.420 --> 27:58.780
They were to be years of menace, bringing into being a new phrase, the Cold War.

